  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Monday)\nIssued at 1209 AM CDT TUE AUG 25 2026\n\nThe radar is clear across Central Alabama at this time with an\noverall more quiet night experienced. Our surface boundary is\nstalled across Central Alabama. It is expected to linger in this\narea through the short term through midweek. In the upper levels,\nwe continue with a low  Eastern Canada, tonight over Quebec and\nsouthward into the Great Lakes and much of Eastern Conus. Through\nmidweek, this low will weaken, but another Central Canada low\nwill cycle through to replace it keeping the overall East Conus\ntroughing. At the same time, there is also a large ridge that\nextends from Arizona to Texas. This leaves Central Alabama in a\nnorthwest flow regime. Tuesday will be another low storm chance\nday with the southeast counties having the best shot at a few\nstorms with the heat of the day. Wednesday into the latter part\nof the week shows increasing chances for convection as the\nsurface boundary moves back northward before fizzling. However,\nthe northwest flow will assist in another front making it into\nthe area in the Thursday/Friday time frame. This will keep\nunsettled conditions along with scattered slightly higher storm\nchances in through the latter part of the week as moisture\nincreases. By this weekend, guidance continues to suggest an\nexpansion of our Southwest US ridge eastward across the Southern\nUS. This will mean a warming trend and more diurnally induced \nconvection for the area, and creeping up heat indices by the \nbeginning of next week.\n\n08",
